<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ophir</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ophir is a land famous for its gold in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, reached by the ships of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> and Hiram of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> sailing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezion-geber">Ezion-Geber</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>. Its location is unknown: scholars have proposed south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a> and the coasts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and an ostracon from Tell Qasile, probably of the eighth century BCE, mentions gold of Ophir.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In Genesis 10, the Table of Nations, Ophir is the name of one of the sons of Joktan. The Books of Kings and Chronicles tell of the joint expedition of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> and the Tyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiram-i">Hiram I</a>, which brought back large amounts of gold, precious stones and algum wood, and of a later expedition by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehoshaphat">Jehoshaphat</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> that failed. The gold of Ophir appears in several other books of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaiah">Isaiah</a> 13:12 speaks of the golden wedge of Ophir. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septuagint">Septuagint</a> also gives the forms Ōpheír, Sōphír, Sōpheír and Souphír. The pottery shard found at Tell Qasile, in modern Tel Aviv, in 1946 reads gold of Ophir for Beth-Horon, 30 shekels.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a>, in the 1st century, identified Ophir with the Aurea Chersonesus, the Golden Peninsula, which Ptolemy placed in Malaysia and perhaps <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>. The 10th-century lexicographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> ben <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a> al-Fasi placed it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. In 1863 Sir William Smith's Dictionary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> pointed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> words for parrot, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> cloth and apes that seem to derive from Classical Tamil, and Poovar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> has been proposed; Max Müller and others identified Ophir with Abhira near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>.</p><p>Tomé Lopes, a companion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe">Great Zimbabwe</a> for Ophir, though its ruins are now dated long after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>'s time, and Milton named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofala">Sofala</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>. A more serious possibility is the African shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-aden">Gulf of Aden</a>, perhaps from the ancient city of Opone in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>. In 1976 a team of the United States Geological Survey and Saudi experts argued that the ancient gold mine of Mahd adh Dhahab in Saudi Arabia, which could have produced 34 tons of gold, was the biblical Ophir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>European explorers carried the name across the world. Peter Martyr claimed in May 1500 that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> identified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> with Ophir, and Benito Arias Montano proposed in 1571 that the name survived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon-islands">Solomon Islands</a> owe their name to reports of Álvaro de Mendaña's voyage of 1568 conflated with stories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>, and Mount Ledang near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> was renamed Mount Ophir. The first payable gold in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> was found at a place called Ophir in 1851, which began the Australian gold rushes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ophir” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…I of Tyre for cedar and cypress from Lebanon and Phoenician craftsmen, sent ships with Phoenician sailors from Ezion-geber on the Red Sea to the land of <b>Ophir</b> for gold, and traded in horses and chariots; the visit of the Queen of Sheba belongs to the same tradition.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-israel">United Kingdom of Israel · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Hiram I, king of Tyre in the 10th century BCE, appears in the Hebrew Bible as the ally of Solomon, sending cedar, cypress and craftsmen for the temple in Jerusalem and joining him in voyages to <b>Ophir</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Society</a></li></ul></section>
